# GitHub Packages registry mapping. Public deps still resolve from the default
# npm registry.
#
# TWO SCOPES ARE MAPPED, AND BOTH ARE LOAD-BEARING.
#
# GitHub Packages requires an npm scope to match the account that OWNS the
# packages. This repo is owned by the personal account **izzywdev**, and there
# is no `fuzefront` GitHub organisation — which is why no `@fuzefront/*` package
# was ever publishable and why the canonical publisher never once fired.
#
#   @izzywdev  — what actually publishes TODAY. The five interim alias
#                publishers ship the same builds under owner-scoped names
#                (`@izzywdev/fuzefront-auth-ui`, `-design-system`,
#                `-security-client`, `-chat-client`, `-chat-ui`, and
#                `-sdk-react`). Consumers still install those, so this line must
#                stay until they have migrated.
#
#   @fuzeone   — where the canonical packages are GOING. The scope now matches
#                the `FuzeOne` org (created 2026-07-21), the account the repos
#                are to be transferred into. `packages-publish.yml` is already
#                pointed at it and activates the moment ownership moves; until
#                then nothing resolves through this mapping, which is harmless.
#
# Inside this monorepo, `@fuzeone/*` names are npm WORKSPACES declared in the
# root package.json. npm links them from the working tree, so they never touch a
# registry at all. That is exactly why the previously broken `@fuzefront`
# mapping went unnoticed for so long — and why `Cannot find module
# '@fuzeone/shared'` (#538) turned out to be a Docker packaging bug rather than
# a registry one.
@izzywdev:registry=https://npm.pkg.github.com
@fuzeone:registry=https://npm.pkg.github.com
//npm.pkg.github.com/:_authToken=${GITHUB_TOKEN}
